(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["principles-of-diagnostic-medicine-i-exam-1-pdm-exam-1-master-exam-form-a"]={"title":"PDM I Exam 1 Master Exam — Form A","category":"Principles of Diagnostic Medicine I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which protein does the dipstick mainly detect, and what does it report on?","choices":["Immunoglobulin, reporting on immune activity","Hemoglobin, reporting on red cell breakdown","Myoglobin, reporting on muscle injury","Albumin, reflecting glomerular and tubular function"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — albumin is the protein, and both are what it reflects. — 6. Urinalysis Diagnostics SV Gopal Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Where does the myoglobin in myoglobinuria come from?","choices":["Damaged skeletal muscle","Destroyed circulating red cells","Bleeding from the bladder wall","The liver, after conjugation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — muscle injury releases it. — 6. Urinalysis Diagnostics SV Gopal Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"What defines non-instrumental point-of-care testing?","choices":["It requires no reagents or consumables","It may only be performed by licensed personnel","It produces a numerical result without any reader device","It does not rely on instrumentation to interpret results"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — urine pregnancy testing, coronavirus tests and fecal occult blood are the examples. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"What are ultrasonography's stated indications?","choices":["Assessment of bony structures, naming the skull, the vertebrae and the long bones","Assessment of aerated structures, naming the lungs, the sinuses and the bowel gas pattern","Assessment of metabolic activity, naming tumour staging, brain function and perfusion","Assessment of moving structures, naming the heart, the vasculature and obstetrics"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — it records in real time, which is what suits it to motion. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"What is the limitation of throat culture?","choices":["Delayed results, taking twenty-four to forty-eight hours","Sensitivity of only seventy to ninety per cent","It cannot be performed in children","It requires a specialised transport medium not stocked in most clinics"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the trade-off against the rapid antigen test's speed. — 3. svDerm, ENT, Ophtho.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Which iron study pattern indicates ANEMIA OF CHRONIC DISEASE?","choices":["Ferritin normal, serum iron normal, total iron binding capacity normal","Ferritin increased, serum iron decreased, total iron binding capacity decreased","Ferritin decreased, serum iron decreased, total iron binding capacity increased","Ferritin increased, serum iron increased, total iron binding capacity increased"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — ferritin is an acute phase reactant, so it rises despite iron being unavailable. — Complete Blood Count and Hematology Diagnostics - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"Which advantages are named for computed tomography?","choices":["It uses no ionizing radiation at all and is described as being the safest of all of the imaging modalities that are currently available anywhere","It is fully portable, inexpensive, and can be brought to the patient's bedside","It is superior to magnetic resonance imaging for differentiating soft tissues","It expands the grey scale beyond the five basic densities, reduces overlapping of structures, works with implantable devices, and allows three-dimensional reconstruction"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — it is described as the cornerstone of cross-sectional imaging. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Which principle applies to throat, sputum and blood cultures alike?","choices":["Obtain the specimen before initiating antibiotics whenever possible","Refrigerate the specimen immediately after collection","Collect three separate specimens on consecutive days","Send the specimen only once the patient has become febrile"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — stated for all three, since antibiotics reduce the yield. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"How often must a testing site reapply for its certificate?","choices":["Every two years","Every year","Every five years","Only when the scope of testing changes"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and the certificate must match the level of testing complexity performed. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Into which spaces can contrast be injected, and what is each study called?","choices":["Joints for a venogram, the central nervous system intravenously, and the bladder for a cystogram","Muscle for a myogram, the pleural space directly, and the ureter for an antegrade pyelogram","Bowel for an arthrogram, the epidural space, and the renal pelvis for a cystogram","Joints for an arthrogram, the central nervous system intrathecally, and the bladder for a retrograde pyelogram"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — three routes named besides the intravascular and enteral ones. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"A patient forms uric acid stones. Which change to the urine is the aim of treatment?","choices":["Make the urine more alkaline","Make the urine more alkaline only if an infection is present","Make the urine more acidic","Leave the pH alone and restrict fluid"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — alkalinising the urine is the management for uric acid stones. — 6. Urinalysis Diagnostics SV Gopal Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which lesions is a shave biopsy suited to?","choices":["Deep dermal nodules and lesions suspected of involving subcutaneous fat","Raised epidermal lesions, basal and squamous cell carcinoma, and superficial rashes","Inflammatory rashes and small lesions where full thickness is needed","Any lesion where the entire lesion must be removed intact"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the three uses given for shave biopsy. — 3. svDerm, ENT, Ophtho.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"More than twenty squamous epithelial cells per high power field are seen. How should the specimen be read?","choices":["It confirms cystitis","It indicates glomerular disease","It is likely contaminated","It confirms pyelonephritis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — that many squamous cells points to contamination. — 6. Urinalysis Diagnostics SV Gopal Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"What mean corpuscular volume defines microcytosis, and what causes it?","choices":["Greater than one hundred femtolitres, from iron deficiency anemia or thalassemia","Less than eighty femtolitres, from vitamin B12 or folate deficiency","Between eighty and one hundred femtolitres, from iron deficiency anemia","Less than eighty femtolitres, from iron deficiency anemia or thalassemia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the threshold and the two classic causes. — Complete Blood Count and Hematology Diagnostics - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"What does a type AS tympanogram show, and what causes it?","choices":["Negative middle-ear pressure with normal mobility, from eustachian tube dysfunction","Restricted mobility with a flat curve, from middle-ear effusion","Normal pressure with reduced mobility, from ossicular chain fixation or tympanosclerosis","Normal pressure with hypermobility, from ossicular disarticulation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the S stands for stiff or shallow. — 3. svDerm, ENT, Ophtho.pptx, Slide 32 (speaker notes)"},{"q":"Reagent strips from different manufacturers carry different analytes in different orders. What does that mean in practice?","choices":["Strips from different manufacturers may be combined on one specimen","Any colour chart may be used, since the colours are standardised","Read each strip against the chart from its own manufacturer","The order of the pads has no bearing on interpretation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — matching the strip to its own chart keeps the reading valid. — 6. Urinalysis Diagnostics SV Gopal Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Why does the radiologist need relevant clinical information?","choices":["The radiologist repeats the study otherwise, so the history prevents duplicate imaging","The radiologist has not seen the patient, so the history is what guides their read","The radiologist bills separately, so the indication must be documented for the claim","The radiologist selects the modality, so the history determines which scanner is booked"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the report is only as good as the question it is answering. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"Bilirubin is found in the urine. Where does the problem lie?","choices":["After conjugation in the liver, or in the biliary tract","In the concentrating ability of the kidney","Before conjugation, in the breakdown of red cells","In the renal tubules' handling of protein"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — bilirubinuria points downstream of conjugation. — 6. Urinalysis Diagnostics SV Gopal Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which agent is used for fluoroscopic swallow studies, and how is the study performed?","choices":["Oral technetium, with sequential gamma camera images taken as the patient swallows","Oral barium, with sequential x-rays taken as the patient swallows it","Oral gadolinium, with sequential magnetic resonance images taken as the patient swallows","Intravenous iohexol, with sequential computed tomography images taken during swallowing"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — real-time imaging is what makes fluoroscopy suit a swallow study. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"In the stepwise approach to an abnormal comprehensive metabolic panel, which step comes first?","choices":["The kidney group — urea nitrogen, creatinine, and the filtration rate","The minerals — calcium","The electrolytes and acid-base group, then calculate the anion gap","The liver group — the transaminases, alkaline phosphatase, and bilirubin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the electrolytes and acid-base group, ending in the gap calculation. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Which quality measure addresses post-testing errors in point-of-care testing?","choices":["Connecting devices to electronic information systems","Requiring two operators to witness each result","Repeating every abnormal result in the central laboratory","Restricting testing to daylight hours"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — communication breakdown is the named risk, and connectivity is the stated mitigation. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"A contact-lens wearer presents with a painful red eye and foreign-body sensation. Which two bedside tests come first?","choices":["Visual field testing, then contrast computed tomography of the orbits","Visual acuity, then fluorescein examination under cobalt-blue light","Tonometry, then visual field testing by confrontation","Fluorescein examination alone, since acuity will be reduced by the pain"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — acuity is indicated for every eye complaint, and this history is a fluorescein indication. — 3. svDerm, ENT, Ophtho.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"How does the Hounsfield table refine the five basic radiographic densities?","choices":["It separates water from soft tissue, which a conventional radiograph cannot tell apart","It separates calcium from metal, which a conventional radiograph cannot tell apart","It separates fat from air, which a conventional radiograph cannot tell apart","It merges bone and soft tissue, which a conventional radiograph shows separately"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — water separates from soft tissue, which is the exception to the five basic densities. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which three conditions are examples of chronic hypoxia?","choices":["Asthma, pulmonary embolism and anemia","Heart failure, pneumonia and smoking","Chronic kidney disease, cancer and dilution by intravenous fluid","Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, sleep apnea and high altitude"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the three examples given. — Complete Blood Count and Hematology Diagnostics - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"Which three characteristics does ultrasound establish when a neck mass is first worked up?","choices":["Abscess versus phlegmon, airway patency, and mediastinal spread","Cystic versus solid, size, and vascularity","Cystic versus solid, depth, and bone involvement","Malignant versus benign, size, and nodal station"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the three ultrasound answers listed. — 3. svDerm, ENT, Ophtho.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Which serum finding supports myoglobinuria?","choices":["A raised serum glucose","A raised conjugated bilirubin","A raised unconjugated bilirubin","A raised creatine phosphokinase"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — it is released alongside myoglobin from damaged muscle. — 6. Urinalysis Diagnostics SV Gopal Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Which is listed as a limitation of point-of-care testing?","choices":["Operator and manufacturer variability","Longer turnaround than central laboratory testing","The need for venipuncture rather than fingerstick","Requirement for a hospital setting"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, alongside expense, difficulty controlling quality assurance, non-standardised vocabulary, imprecision and supply needs. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Can the red blood cell count be used to diagnose anemia?","choices":["It is the definitive test for anemia and replaces hemoglobin","It has no role at all in evaluating anemia","It does not accurately measure oxygen carrying capacity and is not directly used to diagnose anemia, though it is still used to evaluate it","It is the single most accurate measure of the blood's oxygen carrying capacity that is available anywhere on the panel today"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — a distinction worth holding, since the count is on every panel. — Complete Blood Count and Hematology Diagnostics - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"What is the stated trade-off of an open magnetic resonance scanner?","choices":["Decreased soft tissue contrast only, with spatial resolution otherwise preserved","Decreased quality of imaging, which is the price paid for the open configuration","Increased scan duration, which is the price paid for the open configuration","Increased radiation dose, which is the price paid for the open configuration"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — it is available, but the loss of quality is the trade-off. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"What increases lymphocytes, and what decreases them?","choices":["Increased by parasitic infections and allergy; decreased by an acute allergic reaction","Increased by chronic inflammation; decreased by acute bleeding","Increased by viral infections; decreased by human immunodeficiency virus and bone marrow suppression","Increased by bacterial infections; decreased by viral infections"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — note that a virus appears on both sides, which is the point worth holding. — Complete Blood Count and Hematology Diagnostics - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Besides the pancreatic hormones, which organ contributes to glucose regulation, and how?","choices":["The thyroid, through its effect on metabolic rate","The adrenal medulla, through glycogen storage","The kidney, through gluconeogenesis","The spleen, through glucose storage"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the kidney, through gluconeogenesis. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which testing complexity category accounts for roughly 75% of the twelve thousand available laboratory tests?","choices":["Highly complex testing","Provider-performed microscopy","Moderately complex testing","Waived testing"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — usually automated, and the majority of tests in use. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Which test confirms that a raised alkaline phosphatase is of hepatic origin?","choices":["Gamma-glutamyl transferase","Lactate dehydrogenase","A bone-specific isoenzyme assay","A repeat alkaline phosphatase in one month"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — gamma-glutamyl transferase confirms hepatic origin. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What are the two clinical pearls contrasting the modalities?","choices":["Think computed tomography for soft tissue and nerves; think magnetic resonance for bone and speed","Think computed tomography for tumours; think magnetic resonance for foreign bodies","Think computed tomography whenever iodinated contrast is contraindicated; think magnetic resonance whenever contrast is safe","Think computed tomography for bone, trauma and speed; think magnetic resonance for soft tissue, nerves and tumour or intracranial extension"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — both pearls. — 3. svDerm, ENT, Ophtho.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"What is the purpose of the clear tube in the order of draw?","choices":["It is the serum separator tube","It is a discard tube used to fill collection set spaces before the coagulation tube","It is the tube used for trace metal studies where contamination must be avoided","It is the tube used for blood cultures"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — it is nonadditive and is used when no royal blue tube is collected. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"What is the platelet's primary role, and what does that mean?","choices":["Immune defense, meaning encapsulating and destroying foreign organisms","Oxygen transport, meaning carrying oxygen bound to hemoglobin","Hemostasis, meaning stopping the bleeding and repairing damaged blood vessels","Coagulation, meaning activating the clotting cascade"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — hemostasis means stopping the bleeding. — Complete Blood Count and Hematology Diagnostics - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Urine has a faecal odour. Which condition should be considered?","choices":["Dehydration","Diabetic ketoacidosis","A simple lower urinary tract infection","A fistula between bowel and bladder"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — an enterovesical fistula lets bowel contents reach the bladder. — 6. Urinalysis Diagnostics SV Gopal Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"What are the two features separating an acanthocyte from an echinocyte?","choices":["The acanthocyte contains inclusions and the echinocyte does not","The acanthocyte's spikes are irregular and it lacks the echinocyte's central pallor; the echinocyte's are regular with blunter tips","The acanthocyte is microcytic and the echinocyte is macrocytic","The acanthocyte occurs in chronic renal disease and the echinocyte in chronic liver disease, and neither one has central pallor"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — regularity of the spikes and presence of central pallor. — Complete Blood Count and Hematology Diagnostics - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Which organism is a throat culture most often obtained to identify?","choices":["Candida albicans, because of the risk of thrush","Streptococci, because of the risk of beta-hemolytic streptococcal pharyngitis","Staphylococcus aureus, because of the risk of toxin-mediated illness","Haemophilus influenzae, because of the risk of epiglottitis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — most commonly affecting children aged three to fifteen years. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"What are the axes of a tympanogram, and where does a normal pressure peak sit?","choices":["Pressure along the horizontal axis and compliance along the vertical, with a normal peak at minus two hundred millimetres of water","Frequency along the horizontal axis and intensity along the vertical, with a normal peak at fifty decibels","Pressure along the horizontal axis and compliance along the vertical, with a normal peak at fifty millimetres of water","Compliance along the horizontal axis and pressure along the vertical, with a normal peak at fifty millimetres of water"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — both axes and the normal peak point. — 3. svDerm, ENT, Ophtho.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Which three factors influence creatinine and can make it misleading?","choices":["Time of day, posture, and fasting state","Muscle mass, age, and sex","Protein intake, hydration, and recent bleeding","Liver function, bilirubin, and albumin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — those three. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"What causes NON-MEGALOBLASTIC macrocytic anemia?","choices":["Alcohol use, liver disease, hypothyroidism, reticulocytosis, primary bone marrow disorders and chronic kidney disorders","Vitamin B12 deficiency, folate deficiency, copper deficiency, and methotrexate or hydroxyurea therapy","Iron deficiency, thalassemia, sideroblastic anemia and lead poisoning","Acute blood loss, hemolysis, aplastic anemia and infiltration of bone marrow"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — all six as listed. — Complete Blood Count and Hematology Diagnostics - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"For which patients is computed tomography specifically favoured?","choices":["Patients with implanted metal hardware","Patients with impaired kidney function","Unstable or claustrophobic patients","Pregnant patients and children"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — both are named as reasons to choose computed tomography. — 3. svDerm, ENT, Ophtho.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"What causes MEGALOBLASTIC macrocytic anemia?","choices":["Aplastic anemia, hemolysis, acute blood loss and sickle cell anemia","Vitamin B12 deficiency, folate deficiency, drugs impairing DNA synthesis, and copper deficiency","Alcohol use, liver disease, hypothyroidism and reticulocytosis","Iron deficiency, thalassemia, lead poisoning and the anemia of chronic renal disease"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — with methotrexate, antiretrovirals and hydroxyurea as the named drugs. — Complete Blood Count and Hematology Diagnostics - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"A raised anion gap suggests what?","choices":["An excess of measured cations","A respiratory rather than a metabolic problem","An increase in unmeasured acids","A loss of bicarbonate from the gut or kidney"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — a wide gap means unmeasured acids. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 22 (speaker notes)"},{"q":"What comes before an extensive workup on a borderline abnormal result?","choices":["Repeat or confirm the abnormality","Proceed straight to imaging","Treat empirically and recheck afterwards","Refer to the relevant specialist"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — repeat or confirm the abnormality first. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which of the following is an indication for stool studies?","choices":["Chronic cough with sputum production","Fever with a suspicion of bloodstream infection or septicemia","Recent travel, consumption of well water, or prolonged antibiotic use","Unexplained weight gain"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, alongside diarrhea, excessive flatus, abdominal discomfort and change in stool color. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Does a shellfish allergy contraindicate iodinated contrast?","choices":["There is partial cross-reactivity, so contrast may be given at a reduced concentration","There is cross-reactivity only with the iso-osmolal agents such as iodixanol","There should be no cross-reactivity between shellfish and iodinated radiocontrast","There is well-established cross-reactivity, so the two allergies must be treated as one"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the cross-reactivity idea is explicitly rejected. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"How is the visual field assessed at the bedside, and how is it assessed formally?","choices":["Confrontation fields at the bedside; formal perimetry or Amsler grid testing by ophthalmology","Amsler grid at the bedside; confrontation fields by ophthalmology","Confrontation fields at the bedside; fluorescein examination performed by ophthalmology","Snellen testing at the bedside; formal perimetry by ophthalmology"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — confrontation at the bedside, perimetry formally. — 3. svDerm, ENT, Ophtho.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which patient gets an anterior-posterior chest film rather than a posterior-anterior one?","choices":["Any patient in whom cardiomegaly is suspected","Any paediatric patient, because of the lower dose","Any patient having a follow-up rather than a first study","A sick inpatient who cannot get out of bed"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — they cannot stand against a wall, so they are filmed anterior-posterior in bed. Which is exactly why reading the view label matters in practice. — 2026-08-19 lecture recording, 1:14:21"},{"q":"Which four things does a complete blood count without differential include?","choices":["Red blood cell count, red blood cell indices, the white cell differential, and platelets","Hemoglobin, hematocrit, platelets, and the reticulocyte count","Total white blood cell count, platelets, coagulation studies, and red cell indices","Red blood cell count, red blood cell indices, total white blood cell count, and platelets"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the four components as listed. — Complete Blood Count and Hematology Diagnostics - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"By which two processes do the kidneys mainly maintain pH balance?","choices":["Excreting urea and reabsorbing creatinine","Excreting potassium and reabsorbing sodium","Excreting bicarbonate and reabsorbing hydrogen ions","Excreting hydrogen ions and keeping bicarbonate"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — acid out, bicarbonate kept and made. — 6. Urinalysis Diagnostics SV Gopal Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"What must a stool specimen be free of?","choices":["Water from the toilet bowl, which dilutes the specimen","Urine or other bodily secretions such as menstrual blood","Any trace of normal gut flora","Dietary fibre residue"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and it must be collected into a dry, clean container. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"A ratio of blood urea nitrogen to creatinine above twenty to one suggests which kind of cause?","choices":["A hepatic cause","A prerenal cause","An intrinsic renal cause","A postrenal obstructive cause"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the ratio separates prerenal from intrinsic renal causes. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which form of bilirubin appears in urine, and why?","choices":["Unconjugated, because it is bound to albumin","Conjugated, because it is water soluble","Neither, since bilirubin is cleared entirely in bile","Both forms equally"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — only the conjugated form dissolves in urine. — 6. Urinalysis Diagnostics SV Gopal Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"What is a computed tomography image composed of, and what is each unit assigned?","choices":["A matrix of thousands of tiny squares called pixels, each assigned a computed tomography number from −1000 to +1000 in Hounsfield units","A matrix of thousands of tiny cubes called voxels, each assigned a greyscale signal intensity on a fixed scale running from zero to two hundred and fifty five","A series of two-dimensional projections, each one assigned its own radiodensity value as measured in millisieverts","A hydrogen map, each point assigned a relaxation time in milliseconds reflecting the local chemical environment of that tissue"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — water is assigned a Hounsfield number of zero by convention. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Following from how a normal range is defined, what does a normal result mean?","choices":["A normal value effectively excludes disease","A normal value should always be repeated","A normal value means the sample was handled correctly","A normal value does not exclude disease"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — a normal value does not exclude disease. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"What effect does marked hyperglycaemia have on the measured sodium?","choices":["It lowers it, by dilution","It raises it, by drawing water into the cells","It leaves it unchanged but raises the potassium","It makes the sodium unmeasurable until the glucose is corrected"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — a dilutional fall in measured sodium. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 13 (speaker notes)"},{"q":"Serum sodium represents a balance between which two processes?","choices":["Gastrointestinal absorption and biliary loss","Bone storage and bone release","Hepatic synthesis and renal clearance","Oral intake and renal excretion"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — intake and renal excretion set the serum level. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Which study is indicated for complicated sinusitis or orbital cellulitis?","choices":["Magnetic resonance imaging of the orbits with contrast","Ultrasound of the orbit","Contrast computed tomography of the sinuses and orbits","Contrast computed tomography of the neck"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the study named for this indication. — 3. svDerm, ENT, Ophtho.pptx, Slide 36"}]};
